+# Blocked: native async ratelimit API is not available
+
+## What is blocked
+
+Migrating `Client` to `RateLimiter.acquire_async()` — both adding
+`Client.fetch_all_async(urls)` and removing `_ThreadedLimiter` in favour of the
+native coroutine API.
+
+## Why
+
+The premise that `ratelimit` has native async support does not hold for the
+version vendored in this repository:
+
+- `vendor/ratelimit.py` is `ratelimit 2.4.1` and defines only `try_acquire()`
+  and the blocking `acquire()`. There is no `acquire_async()`.
+- `vendor/CHANGELOG.md` lists 2.4.0 and 2.4.1 and neither mentions async
+  support, so this is not just a stale checkout of an otherwise-released API.
+- `vendor/` is synced from the platform repo and must not be hand-edited, so we
+  cannot add the method here.
+- Consequently the supplied tests fail against the real vendored code:
+  - `test_limiter_is_natively_async` — `RateLimiter` has no `acquire_async`.
+  - `test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter` — assigning `acquire_async` on
+    the instance fails, and any `fetch_all_async` calling it would raise
+    `AttributeError` with a real limiter.
+
+Implementing `fetch_all_async` against a method that does not exist, or
+emulating it with the threaded workaround, would only hide the problem and
+would not be the requested native async migration, so neither was done.
+
+## Unblocking
+
+Sync a `vendor/ratelimit.py` that actually exports a coroutine
+`RateLimiter.acquire_async()` from the platform repo. Once that lands,
+`Client.fetch_all_async` can be implemented as a straightforward
+`await self.limiter.acquire_async()` before each `await self.fetch(url)`, and
+`_ThreadedLimiter` can stay deleted.
+
+## Completed meanwhile
+
+- Removed the unused `_ThreadedLimiter` workaround and its `asyncio` import
+  from `client.py`.
+- The existing synchronous `Client.fetch_all` behaviour is unchanged and its
+  test still passes.
